WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.989
Film Courage: Robin, when you came to LA,
what was the first role that you booked and

00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:05.200
where were you in your life?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.410
Robin Riker: Ahh!

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:12.549
Well the first job that I ever got when I
came to Los Angeles was on a television series

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:14.750
with Lloyd Bridges.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:15.980
Do you remember who Lloyd Bridges is?

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:16.980
Film Courage: I do!

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.720
Robin Riker: He was on Sea Hunt and I used
to watch him as a little girl.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.010
And I got it…first of all, I arrived in
Los Angeles with $45.00 is cash, a proof sheet

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.179
with headshots and free place to stay.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.980
And the girl that I was staying with, her
family was in the fine art business.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:41.390
And she was wonderful and I loved living with
her, but I needed to be with people who were

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.960
in the show business.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.900
So I had been going to a school, found a job
and said to my teacher “I’m going to find

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.660
a place in Hollywood to live” (because my
girlfriend lived in Echo Park).

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.080
Found a place with one of his other students
then got this job at this hamburger stand.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.350
Then moved from this hamburger stand up to
this place called Simply Blues, I mean the

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.901
building is still there (it’s right on the
corner of Sunset and Vine in Hollywood) and

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:11.490
I had gotten a job as a cocktail waitress.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:21.790
And so back then you didn’t have cell phones
and texting and all of that.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.850
You had an answering service or your home
phone.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:32.250
And so…a lot of guys would ask for my phone
number and sometimes I would give them my

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.470
phone number and sometimes I would give them
my answering service phone number just because,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.872
you know… I just didn’t want to hurt anybody’s
feelings.

00:01:36.872 --> 00:01:40.420
And it seemed much easier than saying “No.”

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:47.610
And so I had gotten a call on my answering
service from this man named Roe Wallerstein.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.920
And I thought maybe he was (you know) one
of those fellas.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.960
And the third time he called he said “Robin,
this is Roe Wallerstein, I’m over here at

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.150
Warner Brothers.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:03.450
Larry (the guy you used to work for at the
hamburger stand) handed out your picture in

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.979
the commissary and I have an audition for
you.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:08.490
If you don’t call me back, the train is
going to have left the station.”

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:14.620
Well, I am over the moon (as you can imagine)
and I go to my boss and I’ve just started

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.980
my shift, and I say “Listen, I have this
audition…it’s right over the hill at Warner

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.170
Brothers” (You know, Sunset and Vine over
here, Warner Brothers over here).

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.940
“I can go up Highland Avenue before the
crowd even starts.”

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.340
And he looks at me and says “If you leave,
you don’t gotta job!”

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.350
And I looked at him for one unbelieving moment,
untied my apron and said “Well, I didn’t

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:39.270
come here to be a waitress, I’ve gotta give
this a try.”

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:45.970
and off I went and the part was so small that
I actually had to read another character’s

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:49.720
lines because my character didn’t have that
many lines.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.840
But it was a week’s work and it was on a
national television show and it got me my

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.030
SAG card and it was great.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:08.250
The thing that was so wonderful about it was
it was a risk because….I actually didn’t

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.500
lose that job!

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:18.780
I think I did go back a little bit later for
a little while but I had to take a bet on

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.780
me.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.209
And anyone who comes to Hollywood is betting
on themselves.

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:24.270
I didn’t want to be afraid.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.849
I didn’t want to think “Well I can get
another job as a waitress, certainly.”

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:30.890
I did think that.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:34.630
Even if I lose this job and don’t get that
job, I was going to be fine because I can

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.930
always get a job somewhere that pays the rent.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.810
Film Courage: How long had you been here?

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.790
Robin Riker: I arrived [in Los Angeles] on
tax day April 15th (very close to the time

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.870
we’re doing this interview).

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.910
Let’s see and I got my first [acting] job
in August.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:57.730
And I landed an agent by May or June.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.360
And by August I had my first job.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.209
Film Courage: Wow!

00:04:01.209 --> 00:04:03.069
That’s really quick.

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:05.913
A lot of people stay here a lot longer [and
never accomplish either].

00:04:05.913 --> 00:04:11.940
I just want to get back to the whole thought
about how you came to LA not to be a waitress

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.790
or receptionist or whatever (fill in the blank).

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.729
I hear from a lot of actors that they need
these jobs (obviously) because first of all

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:23.639
it’s expensive to be an actor with obtaining
headshots and all the things that go along

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:24.639
with it.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:29.839
Any advice for someone that is in a similar
position because sure this part-time job is

00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:35.599
just to sustain them but you took that risk
[to quit] you felt that even thought you’d

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:37.080
only been here a few months [you were going
to land on your feet].

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.379
Any advice for people in a similar situation?

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:41.069
Robin Riker: Bet on yourself.

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:47.839
If you have the chutzpah to come to Hollywood
which is one of the most unforgiving cities

00:04:47.839 --> 00:04:54.699
and show business, which is one of the most
unforgiving industries that exists and you’re

00:04:54.699 --> 00:05:01.650
brave enough to come to Hollywood, then keep
that courage up and believe in yourself.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:08.270
And waitressing jobs, bartending jobs, those
are the best jobs to get because they are

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.789
the maximum financial return for the minimum
responsibility.

00:05:13.789 --> 00:05:18.830
I used to live in the [San Francisco] Bay
Area, like you…and one of the things that

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:23.800
was the driving force that had me move to
Los Angeles was I was working in a bar at

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:28.339
the end of Sunnyvale/Saratoga Road called
Andy Capp’s.

00:05:28.339 --> 00:05:30.960
The business [at the tavern] was taking off,
it was doing really well.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.610
And I was doing theatre in Palo Alto [California].

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:46.509
And the people who owned the bar offered me
a small percentage of the bar which meant

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:53.869
if I took that then I would have to be responsible
and do what…I’m taking a job and I can’t

00:05:53.869 --> 00:05:55.189
go off and just do what I want.

00:05:55.189 --> 00:06:02.080
So this was my first offer of a really straight
job that had longevity to it and there was

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:09.319
a percentage of the bar or betting on myself
coming to Hollywood with $45 and a proof sheet

00:06:09.319 --> 00:06:14.270
and I chose that because it was what I wanted
to do.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:20.379
If you have a dream or a vision of what it
is that you can or should be doing, you have

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.649
to follow it.

00:06:21.649 --> 00:06:22.819
You have to.

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:31.139
Because the chief thing you want to avoid
at any point in your life is regret.

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:33.110
You have to try it.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.610
Nothing ventured, nothing gained.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.729
Film Courage: Right.

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:46.050
I think anyone who has been around and looking
for those jobs during The [Great] Recession….during

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.580
the recession you couldn’t easily get a
minimum wage job.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.869
You were in a pool [of job seekers].

00:06:50.869 --> 00:06:52.849
You had to go on numerous interviews just
for that one job.

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:55.789
So I think people are so skittish nowadays
and I don’t blame them.

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:56.789
I get it.

00:06:56.789 --> 00:06:57.789
I think it’s scary.

00:06:57.789 --> 00:06:59.639
Robin Riker: Yes it can be scary.

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:07.589
But scarier than looking for a job is setting
your cap for a career in Hollywood.

00:07:07.589 --> 00:07:08.969
This is hard.

00:07:08.969 --> 00:07:13.689
This is a very challenging thing and as I
said, if you have the nerve to come here,

00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:18.699
if you have even a modicum of belief in your
ability to “make it,” in any facet, behind

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:24.169
the camera, in front of the camera, whatever
it may be, you have to pursue it, you have

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:25.169
to.

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:28.219
Film Courage: I think even scarier is regret.

00:07:28.219 --> 00:07:33.689
And I think some people they are not there
yet [in their lives] so they don’t see that

00:07:33.689 --> 00:07:39.369
time does fly by and then regret is actually
the scariest thing.

00:07:39.369 --> 00:07:44.689
Yes, not being able to pay your rent is very
scary but regret is also scary.

00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:51.969
Robin Riker: The other thing is, don’t come
here and rent a $3,000 a month apartment.

00:07:51.969 --> 00:07:57.789
Come here and no matter how old you are, get
a roommate, take a studio, live in a small

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:04.469
place, because all of your energy has to go
toward what it is that you’re pursuing and

00:08:04.469 --> 00:08:07.189
so you have to be sensible about it, you know?

00:08:07.189 --> 00:08:12.749
I mean, you can’t go mad with…this is
one thing that I thought was amazing when

00:08:12.749 --> 00:08:13.819
I moved to Hollywood.

00:08:13.819 --> 00:08:16.409
Everybody spent all this money on a car.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:21.819
Because a car…you pull up to the valet and
the car tells them who you are.

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:27.310
Well you’re just this far from being a valet
yourself, most of the time as an actor.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.899
You might have to take a job like that.

00:08:29.899 --> 00:08:36.140
So be smart about what it is, where you set
yourself up, how you set yourself up, your

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.490
expectations.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:44.120
Bank on yourself, not the purse you carry
or the car you drive.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.120
Believe in yourself.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:45.133
Question for the Viewers: How did you book
your first job in Hollywood?

